      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point on the line that passes through the points \\((2,2,1)\\) and \\((5,1,-2)\\) with an \\( x \\)-coordinate of 4, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the direction vector of the line:**\n   The direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the first point from the coordinates of the second point:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{d} = (5-2, 1-2, -2-1) = (3, -1, -3)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Parameterize the line:**\n   The parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n   \\[\n   x = 2 + 3t, \\quad y = 2 - t, \\quad z = 1 - 3t\n   \\]\n   where \\( t \\) is a parameter.\n\n3. **Find the value of \\( t \\) when \\( x = 4 \\):**\n   We need to find \\( t \\) such that \\( x = 4 \\):\n   \\[\n   4 = 2 + 3t\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( t \\):\n   \\[\n   4 - 2 = 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\) into the parametric equation for \\( z \\):**\n   The \\( z \\)-coordinate is given by:\n   \\[\n   z = 1 - 3t\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\):\n   \\[\n   z = 1 - 3 \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
